#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122217e5-263b-5c2f-a1a6-72cd3fe09ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238207} {:answer/id #uuid "1d352e77-efb7-5062-9266-9d12f3ebc8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238301} {:answer/id #uuid "27dca027-745d-5b20-9ded-c32694cd03a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238400} {:answer/id #uuid "6a625d4b-15d9-5410-ba57-4b7cb980b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238978} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1d6d40-def6-5729-9987-cad555a6c07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27dca027-745d-5b20-9ded-c32694cd03a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238400} :answer-field/id #uuid "25bd6724-359f-5d1b-93b9-7b2ffa2d4f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54529993-0bd1-511f-b362-f0eafc672ef8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105671" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c38294780ac6d5079b759f39a7dbd4944b1c3c21495245dfd314ec180c0434e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/97/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1524a5a3-8447-5f82-93e3-7232aaf62e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409592} {:answer/id #uuid "35e57ca5-117c-5fdf-a50f-85738cb2bbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409593} {:answer/id #uuid "417d24bb-a5fd-5fe7-8f35-35582aaa48b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409594} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd98a2b-6add-530c-b212-292b094d2a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409597} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3bf220-d29b-58bf-82fa-15d03e8e1406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1524a5a3-8447-5f82-93e3-7232aaf62e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ff360a-7344-55e5-9541-0cbd28d2ee59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "656969c6-847a-533c-a9b8-d526f82e1ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105694" :question/problem "![](images/17/17637e938a25e91b9a03921dbbe0f22c8ea760f09cfed9b4a76d07bd5e3b9301.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/f3/f385add60afac473a5734f8c1ed57c6575bada03c9c16b24fed73b200c38e2c5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "613b73ad-b4bd-580f-91d8-dabafde0761e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409595} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf92fb5-00f1-572a-8207-d72045cf4a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409599} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5d28d8-413b-530d-ad6e-efa5df8905f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} {:answer/id #uuid "ee35c795-c099-5c49-977b-052171747158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409603} {:answer/id #uuid "f7113576-7a9c-589a-960b-40c8d014ca07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5d28d8-413b-530d-ad6e-efa5df8905f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} :answer-field/id #uuid "9898eac0-330a-51e3-bb68-1c7f9a46f935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "494f0061-8555-5747-be24-aea11c4501ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126428" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\pi ,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(\\pi ,0)} & =-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0-\\pi \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13264dd3-32ca-5425-ae4a-10ae592d25f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260044} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4d041-01f8-5cba-bbc5-711d818eb654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260692} {:answer/id #uuid "7344edf5-4fa2-53cc-aefd-2082b97c5134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260883} {:answer/id #uuid "deb142df-e54d-5f60-a501-f62bc2ccccc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} :answer-field/id #uuid "6208b069-a1cc-5b39-96a3-5295dfbd12c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84980841-460b-507f-8790-439efc8ff63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126525" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,i+(x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,\\pi /2)} & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} {:answer/id #uuid "64ee45f8-90e8-584e-9ee0-f763b1794d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409596} {:answer/id #uuid "81f3041d-6679-5b2e-8990-3525e4b2226c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409598} {:answer/id #uuid "9811b973-e6d5-574c-9870-5a2db507cfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409600} {:answer/id #uuid "d498b33f-c852-5e73-bfc6-bc624cb1045e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d405633-3d03-5b83-b9d8-c7d039eaf05b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a957b95-e78d-5791-8e30-44fc756d4a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131343" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
